Issues.Body:
### Which @angular/* package(s) are relevant/related to the feature request?

_No response_

### Description

When a field is marked with readonly() or disabled() in a Signal Forms schema, the internal shouldSkipValidation computed signal returns true, which causes syncErrors, asyncErrors, and consequently errors to always return []. As a result, invalid is always false.

```
// _validation_errors-chunk.mjs (internal)
shouldSkipValidation = computed(() =>
  this.node.hidden() || this.node.disabled() || this.node.readonly() || this.node.structure.isOrphaned()
);

syncErrors = computed(() => {
  if (this.shouldSkipValidation()) return []; // ← validation completely bypassed
  ...
});
```


This makes it impossible to show validation feedback for fields that are visually read-only or disabled — a common pattern when displaying pre-filled or locked form data where the data may be invalid (e.g., data from an external API that doesn't meet business rules).

Desired behavior
There should be a way to mark a field as readonly or disabled for UI interaction purposes while still running validation and surfacing errors to the FormValueControl.

### Proposed solution

Add a validatesWhenReadonly / validatesWhenDisabled flag to the schema functions:
readonly(path.name, { validate: true });
disabled(path.email, { validate: true });

### Alternatives considered

Using readonly() in the schema and checking errors().length > 0 instead of invalid() in custom FormValueControl components At first glance this seems viable — since invalid is just a derived boolean, bypassing it and checking errors directly should work. However, this does not solve the problem: errors is also [] when shouldSkipValidation is true. Both invalid and errors are suppressed at the same source.
